LinkedIn News Asia shared a post: Do you find it hard to say =E2=80=98no=E2=
=80=99 at work?

Many employees worry that pushing back makes them look uncooperative or unc=
ommitted. But workplace experts say boundary=E2=80=91setting is about prior=
itisation, not refusal.

=E2=80=9CClarity protects both performance and well=E2=80=91being,=E2=80=9D=
 says Dr. David Leong, chairman of HR solutions firm PeopleWorldwide Consul=
ting. It is useful to lay out your existing tasks and check with your manag=
er which should be done first, he adds.

Jennifer Loh, an organisational performance adviser and executive coach at =
recruitment firm Talent Merge, notes that many employees fear saying no bec=
ause they believe it signals a lack of commitment. But she argues the oppos=
ite is often true: =E2=80=9CWhen you cannot say no, you are not showing com=
mitment. You are signalling that you do not know your own value.=E2=80=9D S=
aying yes out of fear, she adds, can erode credibility rather than build it=
.

Michael Foo, founder of Positive Minds Coaching, points out that boundary=
=E2=80=91setting is often misunderstood by leaders as self=E2=80=91interest=
. In reality, he says, =E2=80=9Csetting boundaries at work is fundamentally=
 about prioritisation=E2=80=9D and when done well, =E2=80=9Cit creates cond=
itions for everyone to succeed.=E2=80=9D
How do you push back on extra tasks at work? Vote in our poll and share you=
r thoughts in the comments below.=20

